1. Problem Definition:
Given array A of length N, find length of longest strictly increasing subsequence.

2. O(N²) DP Formulation:
- State: dp[i] = length of LIS ending at index i.
- Base case: dp[i] = 1 for all i.
- Transition: for j from 0 to i-1: if A[j] < A[i]: dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1).
- Result: max(dp).
- Time: O(N²), Space: O(N).

3. O(N log N) Patience Sorting Algorithm:
Maintain array 'tails', where tails[i] stores the smallest tail of all increasing subsequences of length i+1 found so far.
Algorithm:
function lengthOfLIS(nums) {
  let tails = [];
  for (let x of nums) {
    let left = 0, right = tails.length;
    while (left < right) {
      let mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
      if (tails[mid] < x) left = mid + 1;
      else right = mid;
    }
    tails[left] = x; // replace or extend
  }
  return tails.length;
}

4. Mathematical Proof of Correctness:
- Invariant 1: The tails array is strictly monotonically increasing. If tails[i] < tails[i+1] were false, an increasing subsequence of length i+2 would have an ending element smaller than that of length i+1, which is a direct contradiction.
- Invariant 2: When element x replaces tails[k], it means there exists an increasing subsequence of length k+1 whose ending element is now minimized to x. A smaller tail value strictly expands future opportunities for subsequent elements.
- Invariant 3: Although replacing elements inside tails alters the historical subsequence, the total length of tails only increments when x > tails[last], which corresponds to finding a genuinely longer subsequence. Hence, tails.length is strictly the true maximum LIS length in O(N log N) time.

